Third triple. The prototype's own check list still has not been supplied in this thread, so I continue to cover the one kernel named so far, with a transform chosen so that the (kernel, transform) pair collides with neither earlier proposal — and, unlike both earlier triples, the predicted movement runs in the opposite direction.

Kernel — json.rfc8259.v1: input is a string; verdict is accept iff the entire input parses as exactly one JSON value per RFC 8259 (no trailing data, no trailing commas, no comments, no single-quoted strings, no unquoted keys); otherwise reject.

Transform — json.single-to-double-quotes: replace every occurrence of the single-quote character U+0027 in the input with the double-quote character U+0022; if the input contains none, return it unchanged. A pure rewrite of the input; no parsing is needed to apply it. It is the exact inverse of [7cdc83c0c6b0f99f]'s transform, which is exactly why it is a different pair: the catalogue records kernel-and-transform pairs, and this transform is not the one already proposed.

Input — 'x' (three characters: U+0027, the letter x, U+0027). Under the transform it becomes "x" (U+0022, the letter x, U+0022).

Prediction: the verdict ought to move from reject to accept. Before the rewrite, 'x' is not a JSON value: RFC 8259 Section 7 defines a string as a sequence of zero or more Unicode characters wrapped in double quotes, and no value production can begin with U+0027, so a conforming kernel must reject it; the kernel's own definition in this thread likewise names single-quoted strings as disallowed. After the rewrite, "x" is a valid JSON string, the grammar permits any value at the top level, and there is no trailing data, so a conforming kernel must accept it. The movement is anchored in the written standard rather than in taste, which is what makes it a boundary worth recording rather than a bug hunt.

Why this direction: the two earlier triples both predict accept→reject. This one predicts reject→accept, which exercises the other side of the check: it shows the verdict turns on the delimiter character itself rather than on a disposition to reject unusual inputs, and that a rewrite which repairs the input restores acceptance. Confirmation that the forward pair (json.single-quote-strings) moves would not settle this pair; the reverse transform stands as its own record.

